WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.420
My guest tonight is the author

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:03.250
of the New York Times Best Selling,

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:04.410
Rad Women Series.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:05.330
Her latest book

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:08.010
is called 'Rad American History A-Z'.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.120
She's also a fierce advocate

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:11.190
for social justice,

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:12.400
who's shedding light on

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.930
racism, feminism, politics,

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:17.400
and the responsibilities of white people

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.920
in the world today, as if-

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.400
Wow, that, think that covers everything.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:22.800
I don't think we need
to do an interview now.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:23.730
Please welcome,

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:24.690
Kate Schatz.

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:25.740
Hi, Kate.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:26.811
Hi, hi Conan.

00:00:26.811 --> 00:00:27.680
How are you?

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:29.140
Thank you for being here

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:31.890
and let me explain our,

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:33.700
our trajectory,

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.612
to anyone who may not know

00:00:35.612 --> 00:00:36.445
Yes.

00:00:36.445 --> 00:00:39.220
Right after the killing of George Floyd,

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:41.050
we had two weeks of discussions

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:42.133
on my show.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:44.760
Trying to just open it up,

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:45.890
talk to a lot of different people.

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:48.040
One of the first people I talked to

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:49.970
was W. Kamau Bell, who's

00:00:51.585 --> 00:00:52.500
a friend of mine over the years,

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:53.510
a guy I know

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:55.477
and trust, and I thought,

00:00:55.477 --> 00:00:57.940
"Let's have this conversation."

00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:58.850
And one of the first things

00:00:58.850 --> 00:00:59.967
he did in his interview, he said,

00:00:59.967 --> 00:01:01.570
"Conan, I'm going to

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:04.090
assign you-"

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:05.440
What was the word he used?

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:07.640
A whiteness counselor.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:08.473
I think so,

00:01:08.473 --> 00:01:10.080
I think he said that I
was gonna be in charge

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:11.260
of your whiteness.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:12.570
In charge of my whiteness,

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:14.770
and first of all,

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:15.603
I thought,

00:01:15.603 --> 00:01:17.430
"I pity whoever's in
charge of my whiteness

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:18.750
because,

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:20.750
that is a lot of whiteness

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:21.650
to be in charge of."

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:22.660
So much,

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:23.600
blinding.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.750
Yeah, and then I thought,

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:26.887
I got confused 'cause I thought

00:01:26.887 --> 00:01:29.310
"Well wait, is this person
you're assigning me to.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:33.080
If she's there to help me be more white,

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.010
it's not necessary."

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:35.940
Apparently that's,

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.420
that's not what it was all about,

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:38.870
and we have

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:41.540
started up a bit of a dialogue, you and I,

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:43.410
and I've been looking through your books

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:45.410
and I'm very impressed.

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:47.410
But tell me, when

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.437
when our mutual friend said,

00:01:50.437 --> 00:01:51.990
"Whiteness counselor-

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:53.680
or you're in charge of my whiteness."

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.087
How did you interpret that?

00:01:55.087 --> 00:01:56.160
(laughing)

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:57.040
And a,

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.130
so, I interpreted that as,

00:01:59.130 --> 00:01:59.963
you know,

00:01:59.963 --> 00:02:01.660
it was kind of an offshoot
of a conversation,

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:03.250
that Kamau and I have had,

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:04.490
you know, earlier that week.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:05.470
So,

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:08.430
in the wake of the murder
of George Floyd and the

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:10.380
uprising around racial justice,

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.050
that we saw and experienced,

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:13.770
and are still experiencing.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.123
Kamau texted me and said,

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:18.677
basically he was like,

00:02:18.677 --> 00:02:21.840
"Hey, all these rich,
powerful, white people

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:23.500
with tons of privilege keep asking me

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:24.667
what do I do."

00:02:25.666 --> 00:02:26.499
And he was like,

00:02:26.499 --> 00:02:27.332
"I don't know what to tell them.

00:02:27.332 --> 00:02:28.165
Can I send them to you?

00:02:28.165 --> 00:02:29.750
Is there like a website y'all have,

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:30.840
like for white people?

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.630
Like, what do I do with them?"

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:33.607
And I wrote him back and I was like,

00:02:33.607 --> 00:02:35.090
"Yes, send them my way.

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:35.923
Kamau, of course. Like,

00:02:35.923 --> 00:02:37.200
you don't need to deal
with that right now."

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:40.330
I did not expect the first person

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:42.170
for him to send my way to be you.

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:43.410
[Conan] Yes, it happened immediately

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:46.164
and it's the worst news you ever got.

00:02:46.164 --> 00:02:47.316
No!

00:02:47.316 --> 00:02:48.929
(laughing)

00:02:48.929 --> 00:02:49.762
You know,

00:02:49.762 --> 00:02:51.190
it was great and,

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:52.829
you know, what I really saw it of

00:02:52.829 --> 00:02:55.110
was an opportunity to

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:56.700
do what I think I've always tried to do,

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:57.550
which is, you know,

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:00.010
leverage my platform and my privilege

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.480
to make some kind of impact,

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.940
whether it's through my books or through

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:06.530
teaching, which I did for many years.

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:08.200
Or through just having conversations so,

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:09.123
I thought "Well,

00:03:09.123 --> 00:03:10.680
I've got a lot of new followers

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:13.450
on Twitter and on Instagram, and

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:16.350
I really believe that in
this particular moment,

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:19.060
white people have a lot of questions."

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:21.713
And they deserve to be able to ask them.

00:03:23.381 --> 00:03:24.970
[Conan] Yeah. - They're
all great questions.

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:26.030
No, no.

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:27.000
But there's a lot of confusion,

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.427
and there was and is still a lot of,

00:03:29.427 --> 00:03:31.530
"What do I do? What's the
most effective thing to do?

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:32.597
What's happening?"

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:36.610
Those questions don't need
to be directed necessarily to

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:38.270
your black friends, and colleagues,

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:39.450
and neighbors.

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:40.283
So, I was like "Well,

00:03:40.283 --> 00:03:41.680
I'll just get on Instagram

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:43.890
and ask people to send me their questions

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:44.930
and we'll just start talking."

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:46.140
And I got

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:48.480
hundreds of questions, I still do,

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:52.830
and I've been doing my best
to answer them, honestly.

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:54.590
So many people,

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:56.760
and I'm gonna include myself,

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:59.590
who feel

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:01.680
like "Oh my god, I don't
wanna make a mistake."

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:02.513
[Kate] Yeah.

00:04:02.513 --> 00:04:04.070
I wanna be an ally,

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:07.050
I want to move the peace forward,

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:11.860
I want to be the solution
and not part of the problem,

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:15.800
but I'm terrified of making a mistake.

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:19.110
And that leads to,

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:20.770
I think a paralysis

00:04:20.770 --> 00:04:22.150
that is not helping.

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:23.960
Can you speak to that a little bit?

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:24.836
Yeah, you know

00:04:24.836 --> 00:04:28.270
I like kind of using the term, you know,

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:30.840
co-conspirators or accomplices,

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:32.480
rather than allies. You know?

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:33.680
But those folks

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:35.460
are the people who've made mistakes,

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:39.000
and who've heard the feedback,

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.630
learned and kept going. Right?

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:43.280
That paralysis is,

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:44.113
it's no,

00:04:44.113 --> 00:04:46.320
it's really, really, I think actually

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:48.140
really selfish.

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:50.410
And incredibly unhelpful. You will-

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:52.600
So, the question isn't "What do I do if

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:54.867
I say the wrong thing?" It's

00:04:54.867 --> 00:04:57.310
"When I do the, or say the wrong thing,

00:04:57.310 --> 00:04:59.620
what's the best way for me
to respond in that moment?"

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:01.160
You know, we're all gonna mess up

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:02.630
and it's not just around race.

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:05.210
Men are gonna say the
wrong thing to women,

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:06.790
we're gonna Misgender people.

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:07.660
We're all, you know,

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:10.230
we are all gonna do
and say the wrong thing

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:11.810
and cause someone harm,

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:13.840
so often in a way that
we're absolutely not

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:14.860
meaning to, and that's,

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:17.909
so often what people say is
"That wasn't my intention."

00:05:17.909 --> 00:05:20.810
"I didn't mean that, that
wasn't my intention."

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:23.403
But if something that you
do or say harms someone,

00:05:24.481 --> 00:05:26.070
and they let you know that,

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:27.420
what you need to be able to do is

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:29.100
listen to them and hear them,

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.050
and understand you can
do better the next time.

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:34.500
You know, and if we
just let that paralysis,

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:37.580
you know takeover and we
just don't say anything,

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:41.350
that's part of the large
systemic problem that we have.

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:42.730
The-

00:05:42.730 --> 00:05:44.780
Obviously a lot of people talking about

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:47.050
the book 'White Fragility',

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:49.180
which I believe was written
about a year or two ago,

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:50.320
but you know,

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:54.070
immediately became this,

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:55.400
very sought after book.

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:56.950
After the killing of George Floyd,

00:05:56.950 --> 00:05:59.030
and people talking about,

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:00.350
discussing what's in there,

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:03.420
what I found was really interesting was,

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:04.690
in that book,

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:08.520
there's addressing this idea that

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:12.510
white people have so much

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:14.890
fear and sensitivity about race,

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:17.310
that we're afraid to make a mistake,

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:18.500
and then you think about it.

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:22.110
You think, "Well, a faux pas
is a small price to pay for

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:24.260
trying to engage. Meaning,

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:27.760
if the price that I'm gonna have to pay

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:30.960
to educate myself and to

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:35.350
grow and evolve, is to be embarrassed

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:37.060
a bunch of times.

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:40.230
That is less than the size of an atom,

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:43.040
compared to the Himalayan
mountains, which are

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:45.080
hundreds of years of
racial discrimination,

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:46.700
so get over it.

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:48.297
I think- I encourage people to think,

00:06:48.297 --> 00:06:50.570
"Well, what's the worst
thing that can happen?"

00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:51.680
Right? - [Conan] Right.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:53.830
If you put on a Black
Lives Matter tee shirt,

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:56.440
or you put a sign on your window, or you

00:06:56.440 --> 00:06:59.000
take the step to bring
something up to a family member,

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:00.510
and that's like, you know,

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:02.550
might be uncomfortable or awkward,

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:04.080
or even really contentious.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:05.430
What's the worst thing that can happen

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:06.790
and what are you willing to risk

00:07:06.790 --> 00:07:08.540
for something that you believe?

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:09.373
You know, and I think that-

00:07:09.373 --> 00:07:11.110
What I wanna encourage people to do

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:13.630
is to get cleaner on your beliefs.

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:16.390
If you believe that we have a

00:07:16.390 --> 00:07:19.220
history of systemic
racism in this country,

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:20.310
if you believe

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:21.400
that black and brown people

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:24.410
are disproportionally
mistreated by police.

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:25.652
You believe these things,

00:07:25.652 --> 00:07:27.480
and you believe that black lives matter,

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:28.313
then, you know,

00:07:28.313 --> 00:07:30.237
what are you willing to
risk to talk about that

00:07:30.237 --> 00:07:33.240
and to share that message
with people in your community?

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:36.700
In some ways, very simple and yet clearly,

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:37.670
we talk about,

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:39.890
there's the paralysis and fear

00:07:39.890 --> 00:07:42.420
that we see in this country

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:44.300
about people were to make a mistake.

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:45.150
And then the flip side,

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:46.730
what I wanna ask you about,

00:07:46.730 --> 00:07:48.200
is defensiveness.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:49.033
Oh, yeah.

00:07:49.033 --> 00:07:52.640
It's a very simple thing to
go "I don't know, maybe I did.

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:55.440
Maybe I did make a mistake.
Maybe I was biased.

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:57.660
Maybe, maybe-

00:07:57.660 --> 00:07:59.460
How could I not be biased?"

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:00.970
Let's talk about it,

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:03.220
is such a simple thing to do but,

00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:05.410
people put up this defensive wall.

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:07.020
And it's that defensiveness
that I think is

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:08.680
the hardest thing to get over.

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:10.210
It is, and you know, to-

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:11.043
What I like to,

00:08:11.043 --> 00:08:12.527
you know I kind of like to
turn it around and say like,

00:08:12.527 --> 00:08:15.231
"Well, what exactly are you defending?"

00:08:15.231 --> 00:08:17.500
You know? When you get that upset

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:19.830
because we've had this
conversation and it's,

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:21.350
that's the thing, it's not just about

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:22.970
getting accused of doing something

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:24.760
that is seen as racist. You know?

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:26.890
People will be furious
that you and I are actually

00:08:26.890 --> 00:08:29.170
having this conversation.

00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:30.477
You know, so for those people it's like,

00:08:30.477 --> 00:08:32.960
"What are you so upset about?
How is this harming you?

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:35.440
What exactly are you defending?

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:37.230
Are you just defending white supremacy?

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:40.410
Are you defending, you know,

00:08:40.410 --> 00:08:41.770
that position?"

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:42.603
Yeah, it's the-

00:08:42.603 --> 00:08:44.220
The defensiveness is huge, and I

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:46.113
you know, I really appreciate,

00:08:46.990 --> 00:08:47.823
you know, you-

00:08:47.823 --> 00:08:49.720
I appreciate you having me
on, and I appreciate you

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:51.580
being willing to have this conversation

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:54.030
after the fact, right?
Because, I think that

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:56.430
in all these conversations
that we have about

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:58.070
what white people can be doing and

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:00.780
be better and all this
stuff. I think accountability

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:02.640
and follow up is a huge part of it.

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:04.660
Right? So, we can have this conversations

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:07.360
in the weeks after these
horrific things happen.

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:10.140
But then we, kind of go
back to normal. Right?

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:12.670
Yeah, I was gonna show, like,

00:09:12.670 --> 00:09:13.900
I don't have it as a,

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:15.737
but I was gonna like
hold up my phone and say,

00:09:15.737 --> 00:09:17.220
"We are very much

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:19.970
a society

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:22.440
that's like "What are
we talking about now?"

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:25.960
So, right now it's
Trump's getting impeached.

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:28.280
And that's all anybody
wants to talk about,

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:30.210
and then we move on.

00:09:30.210 --> 00:09:31.280
And then it's COVID,

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:32.360
and then we move on.

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:35.913
And then George Floyd is killed and,

00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:41.040
everybody says "This is now
what we're gonna talk about."

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:42.370
And you think "This is fantastic."

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:47.370
And, it's not that people
are consciously saying,

00:09:47.710 --> 00:09:49.830
dismissing it as an issue.

00:09:49.830 --> 00:09:52.280
But then COVID resurges and we move on.

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:54.320
And then, that is,

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:56.120
I think the issue is

00:09:56.120 --> 00:09:58.080
so much good

00:09:58.080 --> 00:09:58.913
dialogue,

00:09:58.913 --> 00:10:00.210
so much good dialogue

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:03.340
and so much raw emotion was vented

00:10:03.340 --> 00:10:06.670
in the last two months,

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:08.027
that you think,

00:10:08.027 --> 00:10:09.047
"Let's-"

00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:12.890
Sort of a path has been opened.

00:10:12.890 --> 00:10:14.840
I don't think we've ever seen sustained

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:18.453
anger and concern like
that in my life time.

00:10:19.570 --> 00:10:20.640
I'm 28.

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:21.473
Yeah, I-

00:10:21.473 --> 00:10:23.220
You're a lot younger than me so.

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:24.053
Yeah yeah,

00:10:24.053 --> 00:10:25.569
it's just sun damage."

00:10:25.569 --> 00:10:26.760
(laughing)

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:29.450
But, we haven't seen this

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:31.870
and yeah, this is a,

00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:33.340
an opportunity to

00:10:34.330 --> 00:10:37.370
keep the path open, and keep exploring.

00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:38.760
So, you're right, I mean,

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:41.787
I do think it's essential to not say

00:10:41.787 --> 00:10:43.539
"Well, check."

00:10:43.539 --> 00:10:44.372
[Kate] Mhm.

00:10:44.372 --> 00:10:45.563
We did racism.

00:10:45.563 --> 00:10:46.396
[Kate] Yep, right.

00:10:46.396 --> 00:10:49.470
We pulled down a bunch of statues and,

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:51.674
I think we're good. Let's move on.

00:10:51.674 --> 00:10:52.605
Because-

00:10:52.605 --> 00:10:53.770
Yeah, 'cause we haven't
moved on, you know the

00:10:53.770 --> 00:10:57.120
Breonna Taylor's killers
are still walking free.

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:59.860
And there's still people in Louisville

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:01.940
who've been protesting every single day,

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:03.790
for months. Getting arrested and

00:11:03.790 --> 00:11:05.320
not relucting and so,

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:06.920
you know I think
sometimes what happens too

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:07.970
is that for,

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:09.320
especially with, you know, white people.

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:11.460
If it's not immediately in your feed

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:12.730
and in your immediate-

00:11:12.730 --> 00:11:15.000
in front of your face, in your feed,

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:16.140
then you think "Oh, it's not-

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:18.530
It doesn't exist anymore." So, I think

00:11:18.530 --> 00:11:20.660
you know, the level of at which we saw,

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:22.120
a ton of protests happening,

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:24.290
all over, all the time, constantly.

00:11:24.290 --> 00:11:26.150
That's still happening in a lot of places,

00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:28.250
it just might not be
showing up in your feed so,

00:11:28.250 --> 00:11:30.800
you know, be sure to be
diversifying your feed

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:32.250
and paying attention to it

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:33.250
'cause it's all still happening.

00:11:33.250 --> 00:11:34.083
But I think you're right,

00:11:34.083 --> 00:11:35.720
that there is a path that's open and,

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:36.800
this is a time for people

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:38.700
to really be thinking critically about

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:39.900
how do you stay on that path.

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:41.670
Now, that doesn't mean
that everything you do now,

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:43.730
has to be completely, you know,

00:11:43.730 --> 00:11:45.560
all about racial justice
unless that's the work

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:46.890
that you really wanna be doing.

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:48.980
I've been watching
everybody you've had on,

00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:51.550
and you've had great
people on, that I love.

00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:53.520
And you've had some
incredible conversations

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:55.800
with your black guest that you've had on.

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:57.450
Well, what happens when

00:11:57.450 --> 00:11:58.840
Will Ferrell comes on?

00:11:58.840 --> 00:11:59.673
I love Will.

00:11:59.673 --> 00:12:00.720
You know, what does it look like when

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:02.460
in addition to his new movie,

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:03.850
you also say "Hey, like-

00:12:03.850 --> 00:12:05.620
How are you feeling about, you know,

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:07.524
the changes in society. What-

00:12:07.524 --> 00:12:08.357
Right.

00:12:08.357 --> 00:12:10.590
How did the killing of
George Floyd affect you?

00:12:10.590 --> 00:12:12.640
You know, Will or Rob Lowe, or

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:14.180
whatever person's on.

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:16.600
And that's where there's like a real-

00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:18.198
That's unexpected, and
I think that's where

00:12:18.198 --> 00:12:20.070
the needle starts to get moved.

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:20.903
You know, and it's-

00:12:20.903 --> 00:12:23.650
goes back to that idea about
discomfort, like that's-

00:12:23.650 --> 00:12:26.390
White people do not expect to be asked

00:12:26.390 --> 00:12:28.240
about race,

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:30.140
because we don't see ourselves as

00:12:30.140 --> 00:12:31.890
part of race. We often see

00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:33.850
racism as something that happens

00:12:33.850 --> 00:12:35.270
to other people,

00:12:35.270 --> 00:12:37.320
who do not look like us and,

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:38.440
we didn't do it. Right?

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:39.273
But we are actually

00:12:39.273 --> 00:12:40.660
totally complicit and

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:41.993
white supremacy

00:12:41.993 --> 00:12:44.760
impacts us all negatively. So.

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:45.593
No-

00:12:45.593 --> 00:12:46.860
I think that's a really good point.

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:49.200
I think it's-

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:51.750
What's interesting about it is

00:12:51.750 --> 00:12:54.520
the concept that,

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:56.430
when you're talking to people,

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:57.720
and this is something,

00:12:57.720 --> 00:12:59.280
honestly don't know the answer.

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:01.230
But where I'm talking to
people in entertainment

00:13:01.230 --> 00:13:03.450
about their project, is then figuring out

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:05.190
a way to do it,

00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:06.740
which doesn't look like

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:07.780
a "Gotcha,"

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:11.550
doesn't look like I've,

00:13:11.550 --> 00:13:12.530
you know.

00:13:12.530 --> 00:13:14.820
And also asking them,

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:16.060
they may have very visceral,

00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:17.240
strong feelings about it

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:19.590
and they may not be
prepared to talk about that.

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:21.790
So, there needs to be a way to figure out,

00:13:21.790 --> 00:13:23.320
how can I-

00:13:23.320 --> 00:13:25.470
How can that be part of the conversation,

00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:26.600
on going.

00:13:26.600 --> 00:13:28.870
Without it looking like I'm

00:13:28.870 --> 00:13:30.830
trying to make people uncomfortable.

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:31.740
Do you know what I mean?

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:32.640
Yeah, but you always are

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:34.197
kinda making people
uncomfortable. You know?

00:13:34.197 --> 00:13:36.441
That's just my appearance.

00:13:36.441 --> 00:13:37.301
That's true.

00:13:37.301 --> 00:13:39.970
(laughing)

00:13:39.970 --> 00:13:41.790
I can't do anything about that.

00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:43.090
I know, I can't believe I'm

00:13:43.090 --> 00:13:45.870
having to look at this
for so long. (laughing)

00:13:45.870 --> 00:13:47.320
The thin lips and the eyes,

00:13:47.320 --> 00:13:48.900
and yeah.

00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:51.053
No, but I agree that,

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:54.890
that there needs to be

00:13:54.890 --> 00:13:56.760
many different ways.

00:13:56.760 --> 00:13:59.210
Like, one of the approaches that

00:13:59.210 --> 00:14:01.130
I've wanted to take is,

00:14:01.130 --> 00:14:02.630
how can I-

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:04.970
Not even so much as a

00:14:04.970 --> 00:14:08.170
person with whatever
kind of platform I have.

00:14:08.170 --> 00:14:10.340
I'm always weary of my platform,

00:14:10.340 --> 00:14:11.940
because I think my,

00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:14.200
and I think I should
be weary of my platform

00:14:14.200 --> 00:14:16.600
'cause I, I'm not even
comfortable with the word,

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:18.990
I feel like I'm someone who

00:14:18.990 --> 00:14:22.230
has some ability of
being silly and that is,

00:14:22.230 --> 00:14:23.320
brought me into people's homes

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:25.290
for a long period of time.

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:27.840
And so I'm weary of leaning against it,

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:30.000
like the most important
thing to me has been,

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.180
how do I personally-

00:14:32.180 --> 00:14:33.370
how do I evolve?

00:14:33.370 --> 00:14:36.240
How do I change the culture that's,

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:38.930
my ecosystem around me,

00:14:38.930 --> 00:14:39.990
how can I improve it?

00:14:39.990 --> 00:14:42.030
How can I make changes that,

00:14:42.030 --> 00:14:44.030
and sort of lead by example.

00:14:44.030 --> 00:14:45.070
Does that make sense?

00:14:45.070 --> 00:14:46.300
Makes perfect sense.

00:14:46.300 --> 00:14:47.980
You know, taking inventory

00:14:47.980 --> 00:14:50.260
of who's working for you, you know.

00:14:50.260 --> 00:14:51.193
By the way, everybody you work with

00:14:51.193 --> 00:14:52.840
that I've interacted with so far,

00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:55.320
has been the nicest people ever. So,

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:56.623
good job. Love them all.

00:14:57.620 --> 00:14:58.750
I've tried to fire them,

00:14:58.750 --> 00:15:00.530
but they have legal protections

00:15:00.530 --> 00:15:01.980
All right. - which
infuriates me, you know.

00:15:01.980 --> 00:15:03.010
Damn labor movement.

00:15:03.010 --> 00:15:04.330
What I really want-

00:15:04.330 --> 00:15:06.310
What I really want is
a staff of shit-heads,

00:15:06.310 --> 00:15:07.620
but I legally don't know how to,

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:10.173
how to pull it off. I
don't know how to do it.

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:12.350
I'm sure you could find a lawyer

00:15:12.350 --> 00:15:13.900
who could help you figure that one out.

00:15:13.900 --> 00:15:15.710
No, I think that, you know like,

00:15:15.710 --> 00:15:16.820
whether it's looking around at

00:15:16.820 --> 00:15:17.653
who you are appointing,

00:15:17.653 --> 00:15:19.280
who are you working
with, who are you hiring,

00:15:19.280 --> 00:15:21.040
what is your writing room look like.

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:22.050
You know, who you are bringing in

00:15:22.050 --> 00:15:24.330
and what is it like to be
intentional about that.

00:15:24.330 --> 00:15:25.170
You know, but um,

00:15:25.170 --> 00:15:26.590
but I wouldn't just say that the idea of

00:15:26.590 --> 00:15:28.220
how do you bring up those conversations

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:30.030
in ways that don't feel like a "Gotcha."

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:32.090
You know, I again, I think

00:15:32.090 --> 00:15:34.490
black entertainers have been,

00:15:34.490 --> 00:15:35.550
that's what they do.

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:37.380
I watched the wonderful Nicole Byer

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:39.200
when you had her on, she's hilarious.

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:41.510
And she talked both about the pain

00:15:41.510 --> 00:15:43.160
and the challenge of this moment,

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:45.660
and she also talked about
her amazing new book.

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:46.840
You know.

00:15:46.840 --> 00:15:48.133
What's uh,

00:15:49.690 --> 00:15:51.510
great is,

00:15:51.510 --> 00:15:52.710
when I love-

00:15:52.710 --> 00:15:53.570
So many people are.

00:15:53.570 --> 00:15:55.640
We have a writer on our
staff, Laurie Kilmartin,

00:15:55.640 --> 00:15:57.200
who's very good

00:15:57.200 --> 00:15:58.033
at talking about-

00:15:58.033 --> 00:16:00.460
She just lost her mother to COVID,

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:03.353
and she was able to
take that pain, and um,

00:16:04.281 --> 00:16:05.364
and braid it,

00:16:05.364 --> 00:16:08.100
intertwine it with humor

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:10.820
in this way that was like a razor's edge.

00:16:10.820 --> 00:16:12.200
And I think

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:13.470
Chris read,

00:16:13.470 --> 00:16:15.250
from Saturday Night Live was on,

00:16:15.250 --> 00:16:18.810
and he was, you know,

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:22.120
he can be so funny where he's
walking the razor's edge of,

00:16:22.120 --> 00:16:24.930
he can bring up being afraid of

00:16:24.930 --> 00:16:27.080
hearing someone be
racist when he's playing

00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:29.310
a video game, and they
don't know who he is

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:31.470
and maybe someone's gonna, you know,

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:33.003
shout out the n-word.

00:16:34.070 --> 00:16:36.270
And not know that he's there.

00:16:36.270 --> 00:16:37.750
But he was very funny

00:16:37.750 --> 00:16:40.420
talking about that in a way that-

00:16:40.420 --> 00:16:42.010
It's such a powerful weapon

00:16:42.010 --> 00:16:43.690
to be funny

00:16:43.690 --> 00:16:44.780
and bring up

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:48.210
the biggest nuclear bomb issue

00:16:48.210 --> 00:16:49.760
in American history for the last

00:16:49.760 --> 00:16:51.500
250 years,

00:16:51.500 --> 00:16:53.570
and somehow combine the two.

00:16:53.570 --> 00:16:54.403
Those, I mean um,

00:16:54.403 --> 00:16:57.393
it's so many entertainers
are so good at doing that.

00:16:58.990 --> 00:17:01.620
And I think that's a very
powerful tool as well.

00:17:01.620 --> 00:17:02.769
And I think that's, you know,

00:17:02.769 --> 00:17:04.090
it's what I always loved about Kamau,

00:17:04.090 --> 00:17:05.300
and his work is how he does.

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:06.460
And you know yeah, comedy's a

00:17:06.460 --> 00:17:08.400
incredible powerful tool and,

00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:10.280
you can, you know, you can do so much,

00:17:10.280 --> 00:17:12.110
you can go places and have conversations

00:17:12.110 --> 00:17:13.420
that people ordinarily

00:17:13.420 --> 00:17:15.300
will be too afraid to go to.

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:17.300
Conan, I wanted to ask you a question.

00:17:17.300 --> 00:17:18.680
Yeah. I'd love to hear

00:17:18.680 --> 00:17:20.540
what you've been learning, you know,

00:17:20.540 --> 00:17:22.950
you wrote me a wonderful
email and really laid out

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:24.580
a lot of things you've
been thinking about so,

00:17:24.580 --> 00:17:27.190
I'd love for you to tell the class

00:17:27.190 --> 00:17:29.790
about some of the work
you've been doing. (laughing)

00:17:29.790 --> 00:17:31.300
Well, I got stoned.

00:17:31.300 --> 00:17:32.875
I didn't write the paper.

00:17:32.875 --> 00:17:35.300
(laughing)

00:17:35.300 --> 00:17:38.990
Then I wrote something
but my dog ate it, and uh.

00:17:38.990 --> 00:17:41.110
Yeah, no um.

00:17:41.110 --> 00:17:42.370
What, I mean,

00:17:42.370 --> 00:17:43.530
we talked a little bit about it

00:17:43.530 --> 00:17:45.313
but one of the things is-

00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:49.600
I love to read so the first thing

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:52.930
that I did, and I didn't
want it to feel like

00:17:52.930 --> 00:17:54.920
I'm doing a good job
and doing my assignment.

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:56.240
I really wanted it to feel like,

00:17:56.240 --> 00:17:58.300
I want to do this for me, you know?

00:17:58.300 --> 00:18:01.510
I wanna, so um,

00:18:01.510 --> 00:18:03.510
one of the first things I
read and I was ashamed that

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:04.900
I hadn't read it before
because it's a book

00:18:04.900 --> 00:18:06.660
that has been around my whole life,

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:09.450
is the 'Autobiography of Malcolm X'.

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:11.590
And um,

00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:14.940
it is one of the most heroing stories,

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:17.640
personal stories, that
you're ever gonna read.

00:18:17.640 --> 00:18:19.540
It's, it-

00:18:19.540 --> 00:18:22.170
and it takes place in Roxbury,

00:18:22.170 --> 00:18:24.290
which is right near where I grew up.

00:18:24.290 --> 00:18:25.640
And it, obviously,

00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:27.197
Malcolm X is growing up

00:18:27.197 --> 00:18:30.620
in the 1940's, and 50's in Roxbury,

00:18:30.620 --> 00:18:32.210
and then moving to Harlem.

00:18:32.210 --> 00:18:34.700
But that transformation
is absolutely incredible,

00:18:34.700 --> 00:18:37.070
and you can't read that book

00:18:37.070 --> 00:18:39.770
and not see that this person,

00:18:39.770 --> 00:18:42.333
I think his father dies violently,

00:18:43.550 --> 00:18:44.920
at the hands of racists.

00:18:44.920 --> 00:18:48.480
And his whole life is

00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:50.400
all about the color of his skin,

00:18:50.400 --> 00:18:51.233
and it completely,

00:18:51.233 --> 00:18:53.240
and he's obviously incredibly intelligent,

00:18:53.240 --> 00:18:54.320
and incredibly talented,

00:18:54.320 --> 00:18:56.560
and incredibly good-looking
and charismatic.

00:18:56.560 --> 00:18:58.080
And its-

00:18:58.080 --> 00:19:00.260
You see how his entire life

00:19:02.660 --> 00:19:05.180
is changed by

00:19:05.180 --> 00:19:07.190
institutional racism,

00:19:07.190 --> 00:19:10.660
and you completely
understand his point of view

00:19:10.660 --> 00:19:13.650
by the time you get to 1960, 61

00:19:13.650 --> 00:19:15.727
and he's 62 and he's saying,

00:19:15.727 --> 00:19:17.320
"Look, I'm-

00:19:17.320 --> 00:19:20.080
I respect Dr. Martin Luther King, and

00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:21.570
peaceful protest and everything."

00:19:21.570 --> 00:19:23.360
But you understand how he got to

00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:24.410
any means necessary.

00:19:24.410 --> 00:19:25.660
You completely understand it.

00:19:25.660 --> 00:19:27.020
So,

00:19:27.020 --> 00:19:30.120
there's things as simple as uh,

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:31.710
reading a book.

00:19:31.710 --> 00:19:34.880
There's also tons of
conversations you can be having.

00:19:34.880 --> 00:19:37.757
And there are ways that
you can think about,

00:19:37.757 --> 00:19:40.330
"How can I take my little world and

00:19:40.330 --> 00:19:43.573
make changes in it, that will,

00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:46.653
for me and the people around me,

00:19:47.670 --> 00:19:51.660
advance this movement a little bit."

00:19:51.660 --> 00:19:53.940
And so, the question is-

